docs\archive\diaries\T27_T29_FRONTEND_DIARY.md:1145:1. **Palette not the problem.** CRAM dump at register-name: `BG3 = 0000 040C 048E 0EEE`. Pink $040C is present in palette 3, which is what cursor sprites use (attribute `$03`). Ruled out.
docs\archive\diaries\T27_T29_FRONTEND_DIARY.md:1225:- Re-read `src/zelda_translated/z_07.asm` 1948-1973 â€” `InitMode0` dispatches `SwitchBank(2)` then `TransferCommonPatterns` on first entry (before `$00F5 = $5A` sentinel gets set).
docs\archive\diaries\T27_T29_FRONTEND_DIARY.md:1277:Bytes: `01 10 11 00 11 11 11 10 11 11 11 10 11 11 11 10 | 11 11 11 10 01 11 11 00 00 11 10 00 00 01 00 00`. Each byte is two 4bpp pixels (high/low nibble). Pixel values 0, 1, 2 appear. With palette 3 = `0000 040C 048E 0EEE`, color index 1 = pink `$040C`, index 2 = `$048E` (teal-ish). So tile `$1F2` renders a pink/teal 8Ã—8 shape â€” which matches a 8-pixel-tall cursor icon. **If the NES cursor really is 8 pixels tall, tile `$1F2` alone is sufficient, and tile `$1F3` being blank is by design.**
docs\archive\diaries\T27_T29_FRONTEND_DIARY.md:1439:  - `_L_z02_InitMode1_Sub6_FindActiveSlot` tail (~line 3748) â€” `clr.b (VRamForceBlankGate).l` immediately before the final `$0013 = 0 / $0011++ / rts` handoff. This is the "init complete" point, every VRAM/CRAM write is done, so the next IsrNmi re-enables display on a fully-composed frame.
docs\archive\diaries\T27_T29_FRONTEND_DIARY.md:1513:| NES RAM + PPU state clear | `genesis_shell.asm:244â€“248` | 0.17 |
docs\archive\diaries\T27_T29_FRONTEND_DIARY.md:1568:- **Copy path (`_sram_restore` save valid):** `movep.l (1,A0),D1` + `move.l D1,(A1)+` + `lea (8,A0),A0`.  4 bytes/iter â†’ 2048 iters instead of 8192.
docs\archive\diaries\T27_T29_FRONTEND_DIARY.md:1588:Plane B blank fill at `genesis_shell.asm:247â€“255` was 2048 iters of `move.w #$0200,(VDP_DATA).l`.  Converted to 1024 iters of `move.l D1,(VDP_DATA).l` with D1 preloaded to `$02000200` â€” each longword write to the VDP data port decomposes into two autoincrement-stride word transfers, so 1024 longs = 2048 cells, half the loop-body overhead.
docs\archive\diaries\T27_T29_FRONTEND_DIARY.md:1607:2. The NES RAM + PPU state clear loop (`genesis_shell.asm:244â€“248`, ~0.17 frames, already moveq-based).
docs\archive\diaries\T27_T29_FRONTEND_DIARY.md:1639:- `_psg_write` writes directly to `$C00011` (SN76489 PSG) from the 68000 side; no Z80 bus handoff is required.
docs\archive\diaries\T27_T29_FRONTEND_DIARY.md:1658:  - `$92F7-$948A` â†’ `SongScriptEnding0`
docs\archive\diaries\T27_T29_FRONTEND_DIARY.md:1659:  - `$948B-$97C3` â†’ `SongScriptDemo0`
docs\archive\diaries\T27_T29_FRONTEND_DIARY.md:1706:## 2026-04-05 â€” Ear-tuned PSG follow-up (Zelda16.48 â†’ Zelda16.51)
docs\archive\diaries\T27_T29_FRONTEND_DIARY.md:1708:Live listening in BizHawk showed the first-pass PSG math was close enough to be recognisable, but still wrong in the low register. A local note (`apu_to_psg_explained.txt`) matched the symptom exactly: pulse on the SN76489 wants `round((P + 1) / 2)`, not the earlier `2 * (P + 1)` bridge and not the intermediate `P + 1` experiment.
docs\archive\diaries\T27_T29_FRONTEND_DIARY.md:1774:The next listening pass made it clear the remaining triangle problem was structural, not just level or timing. Pulse 1/2 and the reshaped noise lane were both in a usable place, but triangle was still fighting the SN76489's square-wave character and crowded PSG mix. This pass therefore stops pretending PSG tone 1 is a triangle and gives Zelda's triangle lane its own minimal YM2612 voice instead.
