tools\probes\captures\_rev_opencode.txt:39:Furthermore, OAM[10].0 / OAM[11].0 = `$F8` (off-screen bottom Y=248). The NES hides Link's **upper body by Y-position** (pushing it off-screen at the bottom), not by sprite priority. The lower body alone is behind-BG via $20. This is a fundamentally different rendering mechanism from what the plan assumes.
tools\probes\captures\_rev_opencode.txt:73:**Fix**: Step 1 must add a frame-alignment beacon to BOTH probes. NES uses `GameMode==$10` as the trigger. Genesis must trigger on the same logical event (e.g., first frame where a cave-entrance transition flag is set), not a sentinel that fires before the transition. The probes must share a common frame-offset origin.
tools\probes\captures\_rev_opencode.txt:110:The plan asserts "atomic single palette swap" in 1 frame between descent and ascend but doesn't cite asm. The existing NES probe captures FadeCycle ($051C) through 24 frames, suggesting the fade/swap may be multi-frame. If the NES does a multi-frame palette transition, implementing an atomic snap would produce a visible flash that isn't in the NES.
tools\probes\captures\_rev_opencode.txt:148:| Lower body tiles constant | OAM[12,13].3 = $40/$48 unchanged across frames | Walk is H-flip-only, no tile swap |
